decisions/ADR-0028-Files-or-the-graph.content:
Status: accepted 2026-10-15 by Daniel: option B. Graph-side edits on file-backed code are in scope, language by language, Rust first. Sophia’s storage is deferred (below). Claude wrote the options. Related: North Star, Code Graph Schema (the draft that depends on this), ADR-0008 Rust owns the document, JS is a view, ADR-0009 Patches not snapshots, ADR-0012 Two histories, Version Control Requirements, Graph-Native Model.
Context
The North Star and today’s code point in opposite directions.
The North Star:

“Edit a graph whose text is a view.”
A definition is a node keyed by the hash of its structure, and source text is one projection of it.
“Prefer the graph store’s model over the file system’s whenever the two disagree.”
“A file stays the import and export format for a language that lives in files.”

Today:

the code editor edits text files;
git versions files;
the index derives symbols from files;
every outside tool (cargo, rust-analyzer, Julia, pytest, CI, GitHub) reads files.

The flow editor is the one exception: its diagram is the document. It’s saved as its own file and never round-tripped through generated code (ADR-0020 to ADR-0022).
No page decides which one wins for code, and when. The answer changes nearly everything downstream:

what an edit in a graph view does;
what happens when a file changes on disk;
what git sees;
what collaboration merges;
whether Code Graph Schema’s content ids are the identity or only an index.

Three facts constrain the answer:

The ecosystems live in files. Compilers, language servers, package managers, CI and code review read and write files. Unison is the one language that doesn’t, and it ships its own tooling for everything as a result.
Comments and formatting are part of what people wrote. A graph that drops them can’t print the file back the way its author left it. Keeping them means storing trivia (whitespace, comments, the exact token spelling) beside the structure.
Other people edit the same files outside Moonkale. That means another editor, git pull, a formatter or an agent CLI. Whatever is the truth has to absorb that.

Options
A. Files are the truth, always; the graph is derived
The graph (definitions, content ids, types, IR layers) is an index over the files. It’s recomputed when a file changes, never edited directly. A graph view can navigate code, but an edit always opens the text.

For:

it’s today’s model, and nothing breaks;
git and every tool keep working;
external edits are trivially right: the file wins.


Against:

“edit a graph whose text is a view” never happens for code;
structure editors, projections (North Star §11, syntax as a user setting) and e-graph rewrites can’t write back;
content ids become a cache key, not an identity, so history stays file diffs.


Cost: none now.

B. Per source: files stay the truth where code lives in files; graph-native content keeps its truth in a store; edits on file-backed code print back as text patches (recommended)
Each source declares where its truth lives:

files for the languages of the ecosystems (Rust, Julia, Python, C/C++, …);
store for content that has no file form, or is better without one: Sophia’s definitions, a Unison codebase (WP-18), flows if they ever leave their files, annotations and proofs.

One graph spans both. Content ids are computed for file-backed definitions too, as a derived identity: a function moved between files keeps its content id.
An edit in a graph view on file-backed code is allowed when it can print back. The frontend keeps a lossless syntax tree (tree-sitter plus trivia: every comment and whitespace run attached to its token). A structural edit (rename, move, reorder parameters, a rewrite chosen in an e-graph view, a projection edit) becomes a minimal text patch on the file (ADR-0009 Patches not snapshots). Comments and formatting outside the changed region stay byte-identical. The file is still the truth: the patch goes through the normal document and save path, git sees an ordinary diff, and the graph re-derives from the saved file.
External changes:

File-backed: the file wins, the graph re-derives, and an unsaved graph-side edit that no longer applies becomes a conflict shown to the user (Shared Work’s proposal model). It’s never silently dropped.
Store-backed: the store’s own history merges it (a hash DAG, Version Management).

History:

git for file-backed code, as ADR-0012 Two histories already says;
the hash DAG for store-backed content;
across both, content ids let history follow a definition through moves and renames.

For and against:

For:

every ecosystem keeps working;
graph and projection editing become possible exactly where printing back is safe;
Sophia and Unison get a real graph-native home;
it extends today’s architecture (sources, patches, two histories) rather than replacing it.


Against:

two kinds of truth to explain;
a lossless printer per language (tree-sitter gives the tree; the trivia attachment and the patch printer are work per language);
an edit that can’t print back (a projection that changes the meaning of the layout, for example) has to be refused for file-backed code.


Cost: a lossless syntax layer and a patch printer per core language, started with Rust, Julia and Python. A truth field on the source descriptor. A conflict path for graph-side edits.

C. The graph is the truth for everything; files are exported projections
Unison’s model for all languages: code lives in the store; files are written out for tools and read back in when they change outside.

For:

the North Star in full;
one identity, one history (a hash DAG), and moves and renames free;
projections and structure editing everywhere.


Against:

every outside edit is an import with a merge, and git pull becomes “re-import and reconcile”;
comments and formatting must round-trip through the store exactly, or every export rewrites files people share with others;
code review on GitHub sees exports, so they must be stable byte for byte;
tools that write files (formatters, cargo fix, agents) fight the store;
collaborators who don’t use Moonkale see a project that something else keeps rewriting.


Cost: very large and permanent: the importer and exporter per language, the merge, and the stability guarantees. It also makes Moonkale the owner of people’s repositories, which they didn’t ask for.

Recommendation
B.

It keeps the ecosystems’ files and git as they are, which is what anyone outside Moonkale sees.
It gives the North Star’s graph editing a real path: the lossless syntax layer plus text patches.
It gives graph-native code (Sophia, Unison, proofs) a store of its own.
Option A is B without the printer. B can start as A, since the truth field defaults to files and graph-side edits are refused until a language’s printer exists, so choosing B now costs nothing until the printer is built.
Option C can be revisited per source later: a source can always declare store.

Decision (Daniel, 2026-10-15)

Option B.
Structural edits on file-backed code are in scope: rename, move, rewrites from graph and e-graph views, and projection edits. They’re done language by language, Rust first.
Sophia’s own definitions: deferred. There are no real Sophia definitions yet, so where they live is decided once there are some. Daniel’s alternative is to build a base Sophia, even a somewhat non-functional one, to find out what its definitions need. That’s an optional work package (WP-68), not scheduled. The truth: store mode is still built, for the Unison source (WP-18), which needs it anyway.

Consequences

The source descriptor gets truth: files | store. Default files; every existing source is files.
Code Graph Schema:

content ids are a derived identity for file-backed definitions and the primary identity for store-backed ones;
the lossless syntax layer (L1) becomes required for any language that allows graph-side edits.


New work packages:

the lossless syntax layer and patch printer for Rust (then Julia, Python);
the truth field and the conflict path for graph-side edits;
store-backed definitions for Sophia (after WP-18).


Version Control Requirements: unchanged for file-backed code. A “follow this definition” history view across moves, by content id, becomes possible.
Kept:

ADR-0008 (Rust owns the document; a graph-side edit is a patch to that document);
ADR-0009 (patches);
ADR-0012 (two histories).

editors/Linked-Panels.content:
From Daniel (2026-10-14): certain panels should be linked, for example the context graph of a node (the graph locally around it) and the node’s content. Tabs that belong together share a colour, or are otherwise visibly marked.
Today:

The graph panel’s Local mode centres on the active document: one global, implicit link (“follow the editor”), with the Whole view stashed and restored (Milestone 16).
The Links panel follows the active document the same way.
There is only one graph panel, so two documents can’t each show their own neighbourhood.
Nothing marks which panels follow what.

Concepts

A link group is a set of panels that show one subject: a node, or a selection of nodes. One member is the lead (usually the content: a document, a table, a flow); the others are followers that show the subject’s context:

its local graph;
its backlinks;
its outline;
its rows;
its runs and plots;
its comments.


How a follower follows:

group: it shows whatever its group’s lead shows (the default);
pinned: it stays on one node even when the lead moves on (“keep this neighbourhood while I read elsewhere”);
active: it follows whichever editor is active, which is today’s behaviour, kept as the mode of the one ungrouped graph panel.


Several groups at once: two documents, each with its own local graph, side by side. This needs graph panel instances, one per group. The engine already supports several views (window.moonkale.graphViews is a map), so each instance is its own view with its own camera and stash.

Behaviour

The lead moves, followers follow:

opening another node in the lead (a link, quick open, back and forward) recentres the local graph and refreshes backlinks;
the cursor’s symbol in a code file (spec 024) is an optional, finer subject.


A follower can steer, without surprises:

click a node in the group’s graph: it’s selected and highlighted, and the lead’s view of it is revealed if it’s the subject itself;
double-click: the group’s lead opens that node (the group navigates, back returns);
Alt+double-click: it opens in a new group (peek, keep this one).


Selections sync both ways where the lead can show one: a table row selected in the lead is the node selected in the graph, and a block selected in a flow is the node selected in its graph.
Closing:

closing the lead closes its group-mode followers;
pinned followers stay, become ungrouped, and keep their colour until they’re closed;
closing a follower leaves the group intact.


Moving: group members can sit in any tile or window. Dragging one elsewhere keeps the link. A group can span windows (the session bus already moves documents between windows).
Persistence: groups, members, roles, pins and colours are part of the session (ADR-0025 Sessions are the outermost state), so they come back on restart and on switching sessions.

How a group is marked (decided)

A colour per group from a palette of eight theme tokens (--mk-link-1 … --mk-link-8, defined in the theme for dark and light, so the colour check stays clean). The palette is distinct from the source colours (Projects) and from git’s status colours, so a group stripe can’t be read as “modified”.
The tab of every member gets a 3 px stripe in the group’s colour along its top edge, plus a small marker (the group’s number, ①–⑧, or a letter), because colour alone fails colour-blind users and grey-scale screenshots.
Hovering a member’s tab outlines the other members’ tabs, in every tile, so “which ones belong together” is one hover away.
The panel itself gets a faint tint of the colour on its header bar. The content stays untinted.
The tab’s tooltip and its accessible name say “linked with X, Y (group 3)”, so a screen reader hears it too.
Ungrouped panels have no stripe. The one panel in “active” mode shows a small ↯ marker: “follows the active editor”.

Making and breaking groups

“Open with context” (command palette, the Explorer’s context menu, Ctrl+Alt+Enter, and the graph’s double-click with a modifier): opens the node’s content and its local graph side by side as a new group. A setting chooses which followers come with it (local graph by default; backlinks, outline, comments optional).
Link to… on a tab’s context menu: join this panel to a group as a follower, or pin it.
Dragging a tab onto another while holding Alt links them.
Unlink on the context menu ends a panel’s membership; the group keeps the others.
At most eight groups get distinct colours; beyond that colours repeat with distinct numbers.

Beyond the graph
The same mechanism links other pairs, each a lead with followers:

a flow and its generated code (circuit.jl, inference.jl): select a block, see its lines;
a table and the node page of the selected row;
a conversation and the files its agent touched;
a run and its plots (Lenticulum Frontend Requirements LT-12: one time cursor across the group);
a proposal and its diff panels (Shared Work SW-23);
a commit in history and the files it changed.

Implementation steps

The model: ext-api gains LinkGroup { id, colour, members: [(panel id, Role, Follow)] } and Workspace::link_subject(panel_id) -&gt; Option&lt;Subject&gt;. Panels that follow read their subject from it instead of from “the active document”. The groups are stored in the session.
The shell marks tabs: through dioxus-workbench’s Panel::with_class (the stripe and tint come from a mk-link-N class), with_tab_accessory (the marker), the hover outline, and the accessible name. Theme tokens are added for the eight colours.
Graph panel instances: the graph extension contributes one panel per group (graph:&lt;group&gt;) beside the global one; each owns its view, camera and Local stash.
Open with context, Link to, Alt-drag, Unlink, close rules.
Selection sync and steering for the document, table and flow leads.
The other pairs of “Beyond the graph”, as their packages land.

Acceptance (browser suite linked-panels.mjs):

Open with context on Home.md gives two tabs with one stripe colour and one marker;
following a wiki-link in the note recentres the group’s graph, while a second group on Alpha.md keeps its own graph;
double-clicking a node in the graph navigates the group, and Back returns;
closing the note closes its graph;
the groups survive a reload.

Work package: WP-59 in Work Packages.

extensions/Example---Data-Source.content:

                  Target design 



This example uses the planned manifest and Host API (Writing an Extension Part B), which are not built. A working example of today’s API is Part A of the same page.


A static extension (native driver ⇒ desktop/server) adding a hypothetical “CSV directory” source. Shows the source contribution point (Contribution Points) and the lifting rules (Data Sources).
[[contributes.source]]
family = &quot;sql&quot;
dialect = &quot;csvdir&quot;
form = [{ name = &quot;path&quot;, kind = &quot;directory&quot;, label = &quot;Folder of CSV files&quot; }]
platforms = [&quot;desktop&quot;, &quot;server&quot;]
struct CsvDirFactory;
impl SourceFactory for CsvDirFactory {
    fn connect(&amp;self, params: ConnectParams) -&gt; Result&lt;Box&lt;dyn Source&gt;, SourceError&gt; {
        // open DuckDB in-memory, `CREATE VIEW &lt;file&gt; AS SELECT * FROM read_csv_auto(...)` per file
    }
}

impl Source for CsvDirSource {
    fn descriptor(&amp;self) -&gt; SourceDescriptor {
        // family Sql, truth: Default::default() (external source owns the data),
        // dialect csvdir, capabilities READ | TEXT_QUERY(Sql) | FULLTEXT
        // schema graph: one Table node per file, Column nodes with Contains edges
    }
    async fn query(&amp;self, q: Query) -&gt; Result&lt;QueryResult, SourceError&gt; {
        // structured → SQL via moonkale_sources_sql::structured; rows → Row nodes
        // ids: derive(source_id, table, row_number) — flagged unstable (no PK)
    }
    async fn fetch(&amp;self, id: NodeId) -&gt; Result&lt;Content, SourceError&gt; { /* one row as props */ }
    async fn apply(&amp;self, _tx: Transaction) -&gt; Result&lt;Applied, SourceError&gt; {
        Ok(Applied::all_unsupported(&quot;csvdir is read-only&quot;))
    }
    fn subscribe(&amp;self) -&gt; BoxStream&lt;&#039;static, SourceEvent&gt; { /* watch dir via project-fs::watch */ }
}

moonkale_ext_api::export!(CsvDirExt);   // registers the factory in activate()
What the user gets for free: the explorer shows tables, the Table Editor pages rows, the Graph View shows the schema graph, [[sales.csv]] resolves from a wiki page, and the agent can run SELECT against it (policy: read-only source ⇒ writes impossible).
Web users see the same source if the server has the extension installed — the shell routes through RemoteSource (ADR-0005 Server functions as the remote backend).

extensions/Manifest-Reference.content:

                  Target design — there is no moonkale.toml yet (checked 2026-10-01)



A static extension’s manifest is the Rust value Manifest { id, name, description, optional, default_enabled, permissions } returned by Extension::manifest(); a wasm module returns its manifest as JSON from its manifest() export (ADR-0013 JSON ABI before components). Permission names in use: read-sources, write-files, run-commands, network. Everything below is the planned file format.


[extension]
id          = &quot;dev.example.unison&quot;     # reverse-DNS, stable forever
name        = &quot;Unison Language&quot;
version     = &quot;0.1.0&quot;                  # semver
api         = &quot;^0.1&quot;                   # moonkale-ext-api requirement
kind        = &quot;wasm&quot;                   # &quot;wasm&quot; | &quot;static&quot;
platforms   = [&quot;desktop&quot;, &quot;web&quot;]       # omit = all; see Platform Matrix
description = &quot;…&quot;
repository  = &quot;https://…&quot;
license     = &quot;MIT&quot;

[permissions]                          # all optional, default deny
sources = [&quot;read&quot;]                     # &quot;read&quot; | &quot;write&quot;
network = [&quot;share.unison-lang.org/*&quot;]
process = false                        # desktop/server only
fs      = []                           # extra paths outside the workspace
llm     = false
clipboard = false
secrets = false

[activation]
on = [&quot;language:unison&quot;, &quot;command:unison.*&quot;, &quot;view:unison.codebase&quot;]

[[contributes.language]]
id = &quot;unison&quot;
extensions = [&quot;.u&quot;]
tree_sitter = &quot;grammars/unison.wasm&quot;
queries = { highlights = &quot;queries/highlights.scm&quot; }
comments = { line = &quot;--&quot;, block = [&quot;{-&quot;, &quot;-}&quot;] }
lsp = { transport = &quot;stdio&quot;, command = &quot;ucm&quot;, args = [&quot;lsp&quot;] }

[[contributes.panel]]
id = &quot;unison.codebase&quot;
title = &quot;Codebase&quot;
home = &quot;side&quot;
icon = &quot;icons/codebase.svg&quot;

[[contributes.command]]
id = &quot;unison.pull&quot;
title = &quot;Unison: Pull from share&quot;
args = { project = &quot;string&quot; }
llm_tool = true
risk = &quot;mutating&quot;

[[contributes.keybinding]]
key = &quot;ctrl+alt+u&quot;
command = &quot;unison.pull&quot;
web = &quot;ctrl+alt+shift+u&quot;

[[contributes.source]]
family = &quot;custom&quot;
dialect = &quot;unison-codebase&quot;
form = [{ name = &quot;path&quot;, kind = &quot;directory&quot;, label = &quot;Codebase directory&quot; }]
platforms = [&quot;desktop&quot;]
Assets (grammars/, queries/, icons/) are relative to the manifest and packaged with it. Unknown keys are errors (not warnings) so typos don’t silently disable features.

extensions/Writing-an-Extension.content:
Moonkale is extension-driven: the built-in editors are extensions. This guide takes you from an empty folder to a panel, a command, a language and a data source. Reference material: Manifest Reference, Contribution Points, Host API Reference, Publishing and Platforms.



                  Two halves — read which one you need 



Part A is how to write an extension today, against moonkale-ext-api by git tag (currently ext-api-2026-10-12; what changed per tag: packages/ext-api/CHANGELOG.md). Part B (sections 1 onwards) is the target design — a moonkale.toml manifest, a Host handle, moonkale_shell::Tree panels, WIT components — kept so the implementation is held to it; none of Part B exists yet. The decision record for what was built instead is ADR-0013 JSON ABI before components.


Part A — writing an extension today
There are two kinds, and they can do different things:



































static (Rust crate)wasm module (JSON ABI v1)can contributepanels (any Dioxus Element), commands + keybindings, its own settings UI, flow-editor block librariescommands, optionally offered to the agent as toolsseesthe whole Workspace (sources, documents, settings, …)three host calls: list_sources, query, fetch_text, each checked against the granted permissionsshipscompiled into the binary; listed in moonkale_distribution::default_extensions() behind a Cargo featurea .wasm file in ~/.config/moonkale/extensions/ or &lt;folder&gt;/.moonkale/extensions/runs onevery platformdesktop and server (wasmtime), browser (Worker); not on Android yetexamplepackages/editors/image (≈ 60 lines of extension code)packages/extensions/wordcount
A static extension
Depend on moonkale-ext-api (and moonkale-core for the model) and dioxus, implement the trait, and list the crate in packages/distribution — a Cargo feature and one #[cfg(feature = …)] line in default_extensions(); the shell is never edited.

Inside the repository: a crate under packages/editors/ or packages/extensions/, moonkale-ext-api = { workspace = true }.
In a repository of its own (as MathStruct/moonkale-julia does): depend by git tag, never a branch — moonkale-ext-api = { git = &quot;github.com/MathStruct/Moonkale&quot;, tag = &quot;ext-api-2026-10-12&quot; } (likewise moonkale-core); Moonkale’s distribution then pulls your crate by git behind an off-by-default feature. An ext-api-YYYY-MM-DD tag marks a state where core, ext-api and graph-render are compatible (lib-v1 was the first state’s original name).

use dioxus::prelude::*;
use moonkale_ext_api::prelude::*;

pub struct Hello;

impl Extension for Hello {
    fn manifest(&amp;self) -&gt; Manifest {
        // core(..) = always on; optional(..) = on, can be switched off; opt_in(..) = off until enabled
        Manifest::opt_in(&quot;dev.example.hello&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;, &quot;Greets you from a side panel.&quot;)
            .with_permissions(&amp;[&quot;read-sources&quot;])
    }

    fn panels(&amp;self, _ws: Workspace) -&gt; Vec&lt;PanelContribution&gt; {
        // The contribution structs are #[non_exhaustive]: build them with
        // `new` and the builder methods, so a new field is not breaking.
        vec![PanelContribution::new(&quot;hello&quot;, &quot;Hello&quot;, PanelHome::Side)
            .closable(true)
            .activity(Activity::new(&quot;puzzle&quot;, 100, &quot;Hello&quot;))]
    }

    fn render(&amp;self, _panel_id: &amp;str, ws: Workspace) -&gt; Element {
        let n = ws.sources.open.read().len();
        rsx! { p { &quot;Hello — {n} source(s) open.&quot; } }
    }

    fn commands(&amp;self, _ws: Workspace) -&gt; Vec&lt;CommandContribution&gt; {
        vec![CommandContribution::new(&quot;hello.greet&quot;, &quot;Hello: Greet&quot;).key(&quot;Ctrl+Alt+H&quot;)]
    }

    fn run_command(&amp;self, id: &amp;str, mut ws: Workspace) {
        if id == &quot;hello.greet&quot; {
            ws.set_status(&quot;Hello!&quot;);
        }
    }
}
What the trait offers (packages/ext-api/src/extension.rs, every item documented — cargo doc -p moonkale-ext-api --open): manifest, panels (called on every shell render — read signals there to contribute one panel per open document), render, on_panel_closed, commands/run_command, claims (see below), flow_libraries (block libraries for the flow editor, see moonkale-julia’s Lux), settings (an Element shown under the extension’s row in the Extensions panel; write with Workspace::update_settings_in), locales (your strings per language, Fluent key = text files, English required — i18n::lookup, or t!(ws, L, &quot;id&quot;); see Languages and Themes), themes (theme files as JSON, {&quot;name&quot;, &quot;base&quot;, &quot;tokens&quot;}). Colours in your CSS are var(--mk-…) tokens only — tools/check-colors.py fails CI on literal colours.
More that an extension can rely on since Milestone 18:

Platform services by type. Define a type (pub struct GitRunner(pub fn(…) -&gt; …)), let the app put a value in WorkspaceConfig::services, and find it with ws.service::&lt;GitRunner&gt;() — ext-api never has to know your service (git does it this way).
A server half. A server feature on your crate with #[post(&quot;/api/…&quot;)] server functions: the server binary registers them when your crate is linked with that feature (the distribution’s server feature turns it on). Use moonkale_server_host::jail_dir for every path a client names. Shared relays (terminal, LSP, LLM) stay in the host.
State that is not a file. Workspace::state_get/state_put (this machine’s store) and host_get/host_scan/host_write (the store of the folder’s host) with a moonkale_state::Record type — see the Agent’s saved sessions.
Properties on nodes and edges (Node::props, Edge::props), and content-addressed ids (NodeId::from_content) for sources whose entities are their structure (ADR-0015 The core model).
A folder’s settings are data, not authority: whatever your settings section writes to the workspace scope, a folder can never grant permissions, name a program or point a secret somewhere (Security). Offer such switches in the user scope only (see the Agent’s “Allow mutating tools”). Keep state in the Workspace or in signals the extension owns, not in the rendered element: a panel is remounted when it is docked elsewhere.

An editor is a static extension that contributes one panel per node it opens (see editors/image/src/extension.rs: it filters ws.docs.views for the nodes it can show) and claims those nodes: fn claims(&amp;self, node: &amp;Node) -&gt; Option&lt;u8&gt; — the built-in code editors claim any text with 10, format editors their format with 50. When several enabled extensions claim a document, the shell keeps only the highest claimant’s tab (a tie goes to the user’s choice), so a new editor for, say, *.lenticulum.json takes those files from the code editor by claiming them above 10. A database driver contributes source openers instead (moonkale_core::SourceOpener: a name check and an open function), which the app adds to its Openers list.
A wasm extension
A core wasm module built with plain cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --release — no component tooling. It exports alloc(len) -&gt; ptr, manifest() -&gt; packed(ptr,len) and run(ptr,len) -&gt; packed; it may import moonkale.log(ptr,len) and moonkale.call(ptr,len) -&gt; packed. Every value is JSON:
// manifest()
{ &quot;abi&quot;: 1, &quot;id&quot;: &quot;dev.example.count&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Count&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;…&quot;,
  &quot;permissions&quot;: [&quot;read-sources&quot;],
  &quot;commands&quot;: [{ &quot;id&quot;: &quot;count.lines&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Count: lines&quot;, &quot;description&quot;: &quot;…&quot;,
                 &quot;input_schema&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;, &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;source&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;:&quot;string&quot;}, &quot;node&quot;: {&quot;type&quot;:&quot;string&quot;} } },
                 &quot;llm_tool&quot;: true }] }
// run() receives { &quot;command&quot;: &quot;count.lines&quot;, &quot;args&quot;: { … } } and returns { &quot;ok&quot;: true, &quot;result&quot;: &quot;…&quot; } or { &quot;ok&quot;: false, &quot;error&quot;: &quot;…&quot; }
// call() sends { &quot;op&quot;: &quot;list_sources&quot; } | { &quot;op&quot;: &quot;query&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;…&quot;, &quot;query&quot;: &lt;a core Query as JSON&gt; }
//             | { &quot;op&quot;: &quot;fetch_text&quot;, &quot;source&quot;: &quot;…&quot;, &quot;node&quot;: &quot;…&quot; }
//   and gets back { &quot;ok&quot;: true, &quot;result&quot;: &lt;JSON&gt; } or { &quot;ok&quot;: false, &quot;error&quot;: &quot;…&quot; }
The types are in packages/ext-host/src/abi.rs (a Rust guest can depend on moonkale-ext-host with no features to share them); packages/extensions/wordcount/src/lib.rs is a complete example and build.sh installs it. The user grants the permissions in the Extensions panel; until then the host refuses the calls.

Part B — the target design (not built)
1. What an extension is
An extension = a manifest (moonkale.toml, declarative) + code (Rust, optionally compiled to a WASM component). The manifest is read first and drives the UI before your code runs; code is activated lazily.
